 flakes of his flesh are joined together:¦layout ‚¤textÙ2They are firm in themselves; they cannot be moved.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ His heart is as firm as a stone;¦layout ‚¤textÙ0Yea, as hard as a piece of the nether millstone.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter)«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ2When he raiseth up himself, the mighty are afraid:¦layout ‚¤textÙ.By reason of breakings they purify themselves.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ0The sword of him that layeth at him cannot hold:¦layout ‚¤textÙ'The spear, the dart, nor the habergeon.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text»He esteemeth iron as straw,¦layout ‚¤text¹And brass as rotten wood.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text¿The arrow cannot make him flee:¦layout ‚¤textÙ-Slingstones are turned with him into stubble.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text½Darts are counted as stubble:¦layout ‚¤textÙ&He laugheth at the shaking of a spear.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter)«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤text»Sharp stones are under him:¦layout ‚¤textÙ0He spreadeth sharp pointed things upon the mire.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ&He maketh the deep to boil like a pot:¦layout ‚¤textÙ)He maketh the sea like a pot of ointment.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ$He maketh a path to shine after him;¦layout ‚¤textÙ%One would think the deep to be hoary.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse …¤book§chapter)«first_verse!ªlast_verse"¦verses’„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!Upon earth there is not his like,¦layout ‚¤text¹Who is made without fear.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text½He beholdeth all high things:¦layout ‚¤textÙ,He is a king over all the children of pride.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse"